The housing data based on characteristics like occupancy, location, and income were found thanks to the clustering technique.
 It shown that properties are not dispersed at random but rather form significant clusters that reflect various neighborhood
 types or housing situations. For instance, whereas some clusters mirrored lower-income or more densely inhabited areas, others represented 
 high-income neighborhoods with higher housing prices.
 it shows that housing markets have distinct segments, and these segments can be used to improve recommendation systems or pricing strategies.



Among the clustering methods tested, K-Means performed the most consistently. It produced well and interpretable clusters, 
especially when the number of clusters was chosen carefully using techniques like the elbow method and K-Means also 
provided easy interpretation and visualization
DBSCAN, while useful for detecting outliers, did not perform as well in this dataset because the data did not have clear density based separations.

Clustering can significantly enhance recommendation systems by grouping similar properties or users together. Instead of comparing every property with every other property, the system can focus within clusters, which improves both efficiency and relevance.In this scenario, clustering can act as a pre-processing step for recommendation systems, helping narrow down the search space and improve recommendation quality.


Clustering provides a strong foundation for understanding the structure of housing data. While it is not a complete recommendation solutionit plays.This is an important role in improving both the efficiency and quality of recommendation systems when combined with other methods like hybrid recommenders.